Specification ImportCsv

Description Reading a CSV file into an account, and the saved mapping that says
\ what its columns mean.
Details
\ A CSV file has no standard. Every bank picks its own column headings,
\ its own date order and its own way of signing an amount. The headings
\ are therefore matched to one fixed set of fields, and the result is a
\ named profile: set up once per bank, recognised by its headings
\ thereafter.
\
\ A CSV file usually has no identifier per transaction, so test one of
\ ImportOfx -- the one that makes a second read of a QFX do nothing --
\ has nothing to work on. Everything below it does: the tests are the
\ same tests, over the same date and amount, counted the same way. Where
\ a file does carry a reference of its own it is kept and used, under
\ the name of the source that gave it, exactly as a FITID is. See how a
\ downloaded transaction is identified in ImportOfx; what is here is the
\ same rule read for a file without identifiers.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The mapping
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Entity CsvProfile
Description What the columns of one institution CSV file mean.
Details A column is named by its heading when the file has a header row, and by
\ its position, counting from one, when it does not.
| Attribute     | Type          | Default  | Notes                             |
| Name          | Text          |          | Shown in the import dialog        |
| HasHeader     | Boolean       | true     |                                   |
| Delimiter     | CsvDelimiter  | Comma    |                                   |
| DateColumn    | Text          |          |                                   |
| DateOrder     | DateOrder     | MDY      | Not guessed; stated               |
| PayeeColumn   | Text          | none     |                                   |
| MemoColumn    | Text          | none     |                                   |
| CheckColumn   | Text          | none     |                                   |
| CategoryColumn | Text         | none     | For a file that carries categories |
| AmountStyle   | AmountStyle   | Signed   |                                   |
| AmountColumn  | Text          | none     | For the Signed style              |
| BalanceColumn | Text          | none     | A running balance, to check against |
| DebitColumn   | Text          | none     | For the two-column styles         |
| CreditColumn  | Text          | none     | For the two-column styles         |
| OutwardSign   | OutwardSign   | Negative | What money leaving looks like     |
| SkipRows      | Integer       | 0        | Preamble lines before the header  |

DataType CsvDelimiter
Description The character between the fields of a row.
Examples: EnumerationValues
| Value     | Notes                        |
| Comma     |                              |
| Semicolon | Common where the comma is a decimal separator |
| Tab       |                              |
| Pipe      |                              |

DataType DateOrder
Description Which of the first two numbers in a date is the month.
Details Stated in the profile rather than detected. A file whose dates are all
\ before the thirteenth of the month is genuinely ambiguous, and guessing
\ wrong silently moves twenty years of history by up to eleven months.
Examples: EnumerationValues
| Value | Notes                      |
| MDY   | 03/04/2024 is 4 March      |
| DMY   | 03/04/2024 is 3 April      |
| YMD   | 2024-03-04 is 4 March      |

DataType AmountStyle
Description How the file expresses the amount.
Examples: EnumerationValues
| Value        | Notes                                                    |
| Signed       | One column, with a sign                                  |
| DebitCredit  | Two columns, at most one of them filled on any row       |
| SignedPaired | Two columns, both already signed; they are added         |

DataType OutwardSign
Description What money leaving the account looks like in the file.
Details A statement written from the point of view of the bank has the signs the
\ other way round from one written for the customer, and both exist.
Examples: EnumerationValues
| Value    | Notes                                              |
| Negative | A payment is -45.00; the usual case                |
| Positive | A payment is 45.00 and a deposit is -45.00         |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Matching the headers of the file to the fields the program needs
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Description Every bank names its columns differently, so the headers of the file
\ are matched to one fixed set of fields. That set is below, and it is
\ the only vocabulary the rest of the import speaks.

DataType CanonicalField
Description The fields an imported row can supply. Nothing outside this set is
\ read, and a header matched to none of them is carried no further.
Examples: EnumerationValues
| Value   | Notes                                                           |
| Date    | Required                                                        |
| Payee   | Required; the description the bank gives                        |
| Amount  | One signed column; required unless Debit and Credit are present |
| Debit   | Money out; required with Credit unless Amount is present        |
| Credit  | Money in                                                        |
| Memo    | Optional                                                        |
| CheckNo | Optional                                                        |
| Category | Optional; present on an export from accounting software        |
| Balance | Optional; used to check the import, never stored                |
| Ignore  | Matched deliberately to nothing                                 |

BusinessRule Which fields a file must supply
Description A file missing a required field cannot be imported, because nothing
\ downstream can invent a date or an amount.
Examples: FieldRequirement
| Field    | Requirement                              |
| Date     | Always                                   |
| Payee    | Always                                   |
| Amount   | Unless both Debit and Credit are matched |
| Debit    | Unless Amount is matched                 |
| Credit   | Unless Amount is matched                 |
| Memo     | Never                                    |
| CheckNo  | Never                                    |
| Category | Never                                    |
| Balance  | Never                                    |

Attributes FieldRequirement
| Attribute   | Type           | Default |
| Field       | CanonicalField | Date    |
| Requirement | String         |         |

Entity HeaderMatch
Description One column of the file, and the field it was matched to.
| Attribute    | Type           | Default | Notes                            |
| Position     | Integer        | 0       | Counting from one                |
| SourceHeader | Text           | none    | As written in the file           |
| Field        | CanonicalField | Ignore  |                                  |
| MatchedBy    | MatchedBy      | Alias   | How the match was arrived at     |

Collection HeaderMatches
| DataType    | Minimum | Maximum | Notes                        |
| HeaderMatch | 0       | 200     | One per column of the file   |

DataType MatchedBy
Description How a column came to be matched to its field.
Details The difference matters to the user: a match from a saved profile has
\ been confirmed before, and one from the alias table has not.
Examples: EnumerationValues
| Value   | Notes                                                    |
| Profile | A saved profile for this bank said so                    |
| Alias   | The header is a known spelling of that field             |
| User    | Chosen in the import dialog                              |
| None    | Nothing matched it; it is ignored                        |

BusinessRule How a header is normalised before it is matched
Description Case, spaces and punctuation differ between exports of the same
\ bank, so they are removed before comparing. What is left is
\ letters and digits.
Examples: HeaderNormalisation
| SourceHeader         | Normalised        | Notes                     |
| Date                 | date              |                           |
| DATE                 | date              | Case removed              |
| Posted Date          | posteddate        | Space removed             |
| Post Date            | postdate          | A different header        |
| Check #              | check             | Punctuation removed       |
| Check Number         | checknumber       | Still a different header  |
| Transaction Amount   | transactionamount |                           |
| "Amount"             | amount            | Quotes are not part of it |
| Amount (USD)         | amountusd         |                           |
|                      |                   | An unnamed column         |

Attributes HeaderNormalisation
| Attribute    | Type   | Default |
| SourceHeader | Text   | none    |
| Normalised   | Text   | none    |
| Notes        | String | none    |

BusinessRule Known spellings of each field
Description The alias table. It is what lets a file from a bank not seen before
\ import with nothing to set up, and it is the starting point the
\ import dialog offers when something is unmatched.
Details Headers are listed in their normalised form. The table is data, not
\ code: a spelling added here is the whole of adding support for
\ another bank that happens to use it.
Examples: HeaderAlias
| Normalised           | Field    |
| date                 | Date     |
| transactiondate      | Date     |
| posteddate           | Date     |
| postdate             | Date     |
| postingdate          | Date     |
| transdate            | Date     |
| effectivedate        | Date     |
| payee                | Payee    |
| description          | Payee    |
| name                 | Payee    |
| merchant             | Payee    |
| merchantname         | Payee    |
| transactiondescription | Payee  |
| originaldescription  | Payee    |
| amount               | Amount   |
| transactionamount    | Amount   |
| amountusd            | Amount   |
| netamount            | Amount   |
| debit                | Debit    |
| debitamount          | Debit    |
| withdrawal           | Debit    |
| withdrawals          | Debit    |
| withdrawalamount     | Debit    |
| amountdebit          | Debit    |
| paidout              | Debit    |
| credit               | Credit   |
| creditamount         | Credit   |
| deposit              | Credit   |
| deposits             | Credit   |
| depositamount        | Credit   |
| amountcredit         | Credit   |
| paidin               | Credit   |
| memo                 | Memo     |
| notes                | Memo     |
| note                 | Memo     |
| extendeddescription  | Memo     |
| additionalinfo       | Memo     |
| check                | CheckNo  |
| checknumber          | CheckNo  |
| checkno              | CheckNo  |
| serialnumber         | CheckNo  |
| referencenumber      | CheckNo  |
| category             | Category |
| balance              | Balance  |
| runningbalance       | Balance  |
| ledgerbalance        | Balance  |
| runningbal           | Balance  |
| type                 | Ignore   |
| transactiontype      | Ignore   |
| status               | Ignore   |
| currency             | Ignore   |
| accountnumber        | Ignore   |
| accountname          | Ignore   |

Attributes HeaderAlias
| Attribute  | Type           | Default |
| Normalised | Text           |         |
| Field      | CanonicalField | Ignore  |

Scenario A file whose headers are all known imports with nothing to set up
Description This is the common case and it should cost the user nothing.
Given the file contains
"""
Posted Date,Description,Amount,Running Balance
01/15/2024,HARRIS TEETER #231,-45.00,1705.00
"""
When headers matched
Then header matches are : HeaderMatches
| Position | SourceHeader    | Field   | MatchedBy |
| 1        | Posted Date     | Date    | Alias     |
| 2        | Description     | Payee   | Alias     |
| 3        | Amount          | Amount  | Alias     |
| 4        | Running Balance | Balance | Alias     |
Then header matching is complete : HeaderMatchResult Vertical
| Complete       | true |
| MissingFields  | 0    |
| UnmatchedCount | 0    |
| AmbiguousCount | 0    |

Attributes HeaderMatchResult
| Attribute      | Type    | Default |
| Complete       | Boolean | false   |
| MissingFields  | Integer | 0       |
| UnmatchedCount | Integer | 0       |
| AmbiguousCount | Integer | 0       |

Scenario A file with separate debit and credit headers is matched the same way
Given the file contains
"""
Trans Date,Payee,Withdrawals,Deposits
01/15/2024,HARRIS TEETER,45.00,
"""
When headers matched
Then header matches are : HeaderMatches
| Position | SourceHeader | Field  | MatchedBy |
| 1        | Trans Date   | Date   | Alias     |
| 2        | Payee        | Payee  | Alias     |
| 3        | Withdrawals  | Debit  | Alias     |
| 4        | Deposits     | Credit | Alias     |
Then amount style follows from the matches : AmountStyleChoice Vertical
| AmountStyle | DebitCredit |

Attributes AmountStyleChoice
| Attribute   | Type        | Default |
| AmountStyle | AmountStyle | Signed  |

BusinessRule The amount style follows from which amount fields matched
Description The user does not choose it; the headers already said.
Examples: AmountStyleFromFields
| AmountMatched | DebitMatched | CreditMatched | AmountStyle | Valid | Notes                        |
| true          | false        | false         | Signed      | true  | One signed column            |
| false         | true         | true          | DebitCredit | true  | Two columns                  |
| true          | true         | true          | DebitCredit | true  | Two columns win over one     |
| false         | true         | false         | Signed      | false | Credit is missing            |
| false         | false        | true          | Signed      | false | Debit is missing             |
| false         | false        | false         | Signed      | false | No amount at all             |

Attributes AmountStyleFromFields
| Attribute     | Type        | Default |
| AmountMatched | Boolean     | false   |
| DebitMatched  | Boolean     | false   |
| CreditMatched | Boolean     | false   |
| AmountStyle   | AmountStyle | Signed  |
| Valid         | Boolean     | true    |
| Notes         | String      | none    |

Scenario An unknown header is left unmatched and asked about
Description The file still imports: the unmatched column is simply not read.
\ The user is told which column was not understood, and may map it
\ or leave it, and the choice is saved with the profile.
Given the file contains
"""
Date,Narrative,Amount,Reference
01/15/2024,HARRIS TEETER,-45.00,ABC123
"""
When headers matched
Then header matches are : HeaderMatches
| Position | SourceHeader | Field  | MatchedBy |
| 1        | Date         | Date   | Alias     |
| 2        | Narrative    | Ignore | None      |
| 3        | Amount       | Amount | Alias     |
| 4        | Reference    | Ignore | None      |
Then header matching is complete : HeaderMatchResult Vertical
| Complete       | false |
| MissingFields  | 1     |
| UnmatchedCount | 2     |
| AmbiguousCount | 0     |
Then user asked about headers are : UnmatchedHeader
| Position | SourceHeader | Suggestion |
| 2        | Narrative    | Payee      |
| 4        | Reference    | CheckNo    |
# Payee is required and nothing matched it, so the import cannot proceed until
# column 2 is answered. Column 4 may be left as Ignore.

Attributes UnmatchedHeader
| Attribute    | Type           | Default |
| Position     | Integer        | 0       |
| SourceHeader | Text           | none    |
| Suggestion   | CanonicalField | Ignore  |

Scenario A required field that nothing matches stops the import
Description Refusing is the only option: a row with no date cannot be placed in
\ a register.
Given the file contains
"""
Description,Amount
HARRIS TEETER,-45.00
"""
When headers matched
Then rejected because : Rejection
| Reason                                                      |
| No column matched Date, which is required                   |

Scenario Two headers matching one field is reported rather than resolved
Description Taking the first would be a guess, and a file with both Amount and
\ Transaction Amount is a file worth looking at before importing.
Given the file contains
"""
Date,Description,Amount,Transaction Amount
01/15/2024,HARRIS TEETER,-45.00,-45.00
"""
When headers matched
Then header matching is complete : HeaderMatchResult Vertical
| Complete       | false |
| MissingFields  | 0     |
| UnmatchedCount | 0     |
| AmbiguousCount | 1     |
Then ambiguous fields are : AmbiguousField
| Field  | Positions | Headers                   |
| Amount | 3 and 4   | Amount, Transaction Amount |

Attributes AmbiguousField
| Attribute | Type           | Default |
| Field     | CanonicalField | Date    |
| Positions | Text           |         |
| Headers   | Text           |         |

Scenario Two columns with the same header are both reported
Description It happens in exports that concatenate two statements. Neither is
\ chosen.
Given the file contains
"""
Date,Description,Amount,Amount
01/15/2024,HARRIS TEETER,-45.00,-45.00
"""
When headers matched
Then ambiguous fields are : AmbiguousField
| Field  | Positions | Headers        |
| Amount | 3 and 4   | Amount, Amount |

Scenario A saved profile is recognised by the headers of the file
Description Once a bank has been set up, its files are recognised by the set of
\ headers they carry, so the right profile is chosen without the user
\ naming it. The signature is the matched headers in file order.
Given saved profiles are : SavedProfile
| Name            | Signature                                 |
| Example Bank    | Posted Date,Description,Amount,Running Balance |
| Two Column Bank | Trans Date,Payee,Withdrawals,Deposits     |
Given the file contains
"""
Trans Date,Payee,Withdrawals,Deposits
01/15/2024,HARRIS TEETER,45.00,
"""
When headers matched
Then profile recognised is : ProfileRecognition Vertical
| Name      | Two Column Bank |
| Asked     | false           |
| MatchedBy | Profile         |

Attributes SavedProfile
| Attribute | Type | Default |
| Name      | Text |         |
| Signature | Text |         |

Attributes ProfileRecognition
| Attribute | Type      | Default |
| Name      | Text      | none    |
| Asked     | Boolean   | true    |
| MatchedBy | MatchedBy | Alias   |

Scenario A profile takes precedence over the alias table
Description The user corrected a mapping once; that correction is not quietly
\ undone by an alias that happens to match. Here Description was
\ mapped to Memo rather than Payee, and it stays mapped to Memo.
Given saved profiles are : SavedProfile
| Name         | Signature                      |
| Odd Bank     | Date,Description,Narrative,Amount |
Given saved header matches are : HeaderMatches
| Position | SourceHeader | Field  | MatchedBy |
| 1        | Date         | Date   | User      |
| 2        | Description  | Memo   | User      |
| 3        | Narrative    | Payee  | User      |
| 4        | Amount       | Amount | User      |
Given the file contains
"""
Date,Description,Narrative,Amount
01/15/2024,CARD PURCHASE,HARRIS TEETER,-45.00
"""
When headers matched
Then header matches are : HeaderMatches
| Position | SourceHeader | Field  | MatchedBy |
| 1        | Date         | Date   | Profile   |
| 2        | Description  | Memo   | Profile   |
| 3        | Narrative    | Payee  | Profile   |
| 4        | Amount       | Amount | Profile   |
Then rows read are : CsvRowRead
| Date       | Payee         | Memo          | Amount |
| 2024-01-15 | HARRIS TEETER | CARD PURCHASE | -45.00 |

Scenario A file whose headers are in a different order is still recognised
Description The signature is compared as a set, so a bank that reorders its
\ columns between exports does not need setting up again. The
\ positions are taken from this file, not from the saved ones.
Given saved profiles are : SavedProfile
| Name         | Signature                                      |
| Example Bank | Posted Date,Description,Amount,Running Balance |
Given the file contains
"""
Description,Posted Date,Running Balance,Amount
HARRIS TEETER,01/15/2024,1705.00,-45.00
"""
When headers matched
Then profile recognised is : ProfileRecognition Vertical
| Name  | Example Bank |
| Asked | false        |
Then header matches are : HeaderMatches
| Position | SourceHeader    | Field   | MatchedBy |
| 1        | Description     | Payee   | Profile   |
| 2        | Posted Date     | Date    | Profile   |
| 3        | Running Balance | Balance | Profile   |
| 4        | Amount          | Amount  | Profile   |

Scenario A confirmed mapping is saved against the name the user gives the bank
Description The mapping is set up once. What is saved is the signature, the
\ matches, and the file-level settings of the profile.
Given the file contains
"""
Date,Narrative,Amount
01/15/2024,HARRIS TEETER,-45.00
"""
When headers matched
When header mapped by user : HeaderMatch Vertical
| Position     | 2         |
| SourceHeader | Narrative |
| Field        | Payee     |
| MatchedBy    | User      |
When profile saved as : ProfileLabel
| Name           |
| Narrative Bank |
Then saved profiles are : SavedProfile
| Name           | Signature             |
| Narrative Bank | Date,Narrative,Amount |
Then header matching is complete : HeaderMatchResult Vertical
| Complete       | true |
| MissingFields  | 0    |
| UnmatchedCount | 0    |
| AmbiguousCount | 0    |

DataType ProfileName
Description What the user calls a saved mapping.
Uses Text
Examples: ValidValues
| Value          | IsValid | Notes                    |
| Narrative Bank | true    |                          |
|                | false   | A saved profile is named |

Scenario Columns the file has that the program does not need are ignored quietly
Description A bank that exports a currency column and a transaction type column
\ is not asking a question. These are matched to Ignore by the alias
\ table, so the user is not asked about them.
Given the file contains
"""
Date,Description,Amount,Currency,Transaction Type,Status
01/15/2024,HARRIS TEETER,-45.00,USD,DEBIT,POSTED
"""
When headers matched
Then header matches are : HeaderMatches
| Position | SourceHeader     | Field  | MatchedBy |
| 1        | Date             | Date   | Alias     |
| 2        | Description      | Payee  | Alias     |
| 3        | Amount           | Amount | Alias     |
| 4        | Currency         | Ignore | Alias     |
| 5        | Transaction Type | Ignore | Alias     |
| 6        | Status           | Ignore | Alias     |
Then header matching is complete : HeaderMatchResult Vertical
| Complete       | true |
| MissingFields  | 0    |
| UnmatchedCount | 0    |
| AmbiguousCount | 0    |

Scenario A matched balance column is used to check the import, not stored
Description The balance after each row is the strongest check a CSV file offers,
\ and it is the nearest thing in CSV to the ledger balance of an OFX
\ file. A disagreement is reported with the row that broke it.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking        | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Uncategorized | Expense | false       |
Given transactions are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Amount  |
| T0  | Assets:Checking | 2024-01-01 | 1750.00 |
Given import target is : ImportTarget Vertical
| AccountPath | Assets:Checking |
Given the file contains
"""
Date,Description,Amount,Balance
01/15/2024,HARRIS TEETER,-45.00,1705.00
01/16/2024,SHELL,-52.00,1660.00
"""
When rows imported
Then balance column check is : BalanceColumnCheck
| Line | RowBalance | ComputedBalance | Agrees |
| 2    | 1705.00    | 1705.00         | true   |
| 3    | 1660.00    | 1653.00         | false  |
# The second row disagrees by seven dollars, which says a transaction is
# missing from the file between the two rows, or the opening position is wrong.

Attributes BalanceColumnCheck
| Attribute       | Type    | Default |
| Line            | Integer | 0       |
| RowBalance      | Money   | 0.00    |
| ComputedBalance | Money   | 0.00    |
| Agrees          | Boolean | false   |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A balance column the profile names itself
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

BusinessRule When the balance column is checked and when it is left alone
Description The check is worth having only where there is something to check
\ against, and a column that is absent or empty says nothing.
Details The balance is never stored and never becomes a transaction. It is
\ read, compared against the position the rows themselves arrive at,
\ and then forgotten -- so nothing here can make an import wrong. The
\ worst a missing balance column can do is leave the import unchecked,
\ which is what every CSV import did before there was one.
Examples: BalanceColumnUse
| WhatTheProfileNames        | WhatTheFileHas          | WhatHappens                                     |
| Nothing                    | No balance column       | No check, as before                             |
| Nothing                    | A known balance heading | Checked, placed by the alias table              |
| A column the file has      | That column             | Checked against the column the profile named    |
| A column the file lacks    | No such heading         | No check, and the import is not refused         |
| A column the file has      | That column, some cells empty | Checked on the rows that have a balance   |

Attributes BalanceColumnUse
| Attribute           | Type   | Default |
| WhatTheProfileNames | String |         |
| WhatTheFileHas      | String |         |
| WhatHappens         | String |         |

Scenario A profile names the balance column when the heading is one nobody knows
Description The alias table places a column headed Running Balance without
\ being told. A bank whose balance column is headed something else can
\ still be checked, by naming the column in the profile, which is what
\ BalanceColumn is for.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type     | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking        | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Uncategorized | Expense  | false       |
Given transactions are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Amount  |
| T0  | Assets:Checking | 2024-01-01 | 1750.00 |
Given import target is : ImportTarget Vertical
| AccountPath | Assets:Checking |
Given the csv profile is : CsvProfile Vertical
| Name          | Odd Heading Bank |
| DateColumn    | Date             |
| PayeeColumn   | Description      |
| AmountColumn  | Amount           |
| BalanceColumn | Position         |
Given the file contains
"""
Date,Description,Amount,Position
01/15/2024,HARRIS TEETER,-45.00,1705.00
01/16/2024,SHELL,-52.00,1653.00
"""
When rows imported
Then balance column check is : BalanceColumnCheck
| Line | RowBalance | ComputedBalance | Agrees |
| 2    | 1705.00    | 1705.00         | true   |
| 3    | 1653.00    | 1653.00         | true   |

Scenario A balance column the file does not have is not a reason to refuse
Description A profile is reused across every file a bank sends, and one of them
\ may be an export that leaves the balance out. The rows are still
\ transactions; all that is lost is the check.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type     | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking        | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Uncategorized | Expense  | false       |
Given import target is : ImportTarget Vertical
| AccountPath | Assets:Checking |
Given the csv profile is : CsvProfile Vertical
| Name          | Odd Heading Bank |
| DateColumn    | Date             |
| PayeeColumn   | Description      |
| AmountColumn  | Amount           |
| BalanceColumn | Position         |
Given the file contains
"""
Date,Description,Amount
01/15/2024,HARRIS TEETER,-45.00
"""
When rows imported
Then rows read are : CsvRowRead
| Date       | Payee         | Amount |
| 2024-01-15 | HARRIS TEETER | -45.00 |
Then balance column check is : BalanceColumnCheck
| Line | RowBalance | ComputedBalance | Agrees |
# No rows. Nothing was checked, because the file carried no balance.

Scenario A row with nothing in the balance column is not checked
Description Several banks write the balance on the last row of the day only.
\ A blank is not a balance of zero, and reading it as one would report
\ every such row as a disagreement.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type     | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking        | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Uncategorized | Expense  | false       |
Given transactions are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Amount  |
| T0  | Assets:Checking | 2024-01-01 | 1750.00 |
Given import target is : ImportTarget Vertical
| AccountPath | Assets:Checking |
Given the csv profile is : CsvProfile Vertical
| Name          | Odd Heading Bank |
| DateColumn    | Date             |
| PayeeColumn   | Description      |
| AmountColumn  | Amount           |
| BalanceColumn | Position         |
Given the file contains
"""
Date,Description,Amount,Position
01/15/2024,HARRIS TEETER,-45.00,
01/16/2024,SHELL,-52.00,1653.00
"""
When rows imported
Then balance column check is : BalanceColumnCheck
| Line | RowBalance | ComputedBalance | Agrees |
| 3    | 1653.00    | 1653.00         | true   |
# The first row is absent from the check rather than failing it.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Reading the rows
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Attributes CsvRowRead
Description One row of the file, after the mapping has been applied to it.
| Attribute | Type        | Default    |
| Date      | Date        | 2024-01-01 |
| Payee     | PayeeName   | none       |
| Memo      | Text        | none       |
| CheckNo   | CheckNumber | none       |
| Category  | AccountPath | none       |
| Amount    | Money       | 0.00       |

Scenario A file with a signed amount column
Given the csv profile is : CsvProfile Vertical
