Attributes CsvRowError
| Attribute | Type    | Default |
| Line      | Integer | 0       |
| Reason    | Text    |         |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Dates
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

BusinessRule How a date cell is read
Description The profile says which order the parts are in; the separator and the
\ width of the year are taken as they come.
Examples: CsvDateParse
| DateOrder | Text       | Date       | Valid | Notes                        |
| MDY       | 01/15/2024 | 2024-01-15 | true  |                              |
| MDY       | 1/5/2024   | 2024-01-05 | true  | Single digits                |
| MDY       | 01-15-2024 | 2024-01-15 | true  | Dashes                       |
| MDY       | 01/15/24   | 2024-01-15 | true  | Two-digit year               |
| DMY       | 15/01/2024 | 2024-01-15 | true  |                              |
| DMY       | 03/04/2024 | 2024-04-03 | true  | The same text, read the other way |
| MDY       | 03/04/2024 | 2024-03-04 | true  |                              |
| YMD       | 2024-01-15 | 2024-01-15 | true  |                              |
| YMD       | 2024/01/15 | 2024-01-15 | true  |                              |
| MDY       | 15/01/2024 | 2024-01-01 | false | No fifteenth month           |
| MDY       | 02/30/2024 | 2024-01-01 | false | February has no thirtieth    |
| MDY       | 02/29/2024 | 2024-02-29 | true  | 2024 is a leap year          |
| MDY       | 02/29/2023 | 2024-01-01 | false | 2023 is not                  |
| MDY       |            | 2024-01-01 | false | A date is required           |

Attributes CsvDateParse
| Attribute | Type      | Default    |
| DateOrder | DateOrder | MDY        |
| Text      | String    | none       |
| Date      | Date      | 2024-01-01 |
| Valid     | Boolean   | true       |
| Notes     | String    | none       |

BusinessRule How a two-digit year is read
Description A window is needed, and twenty years of history sets where it goes.
Details Years 70 to 99 are nineteen hundreds; 00 to 69 are two thousands. A
\ file old enough to write dates this way will not contain 2070.
Examples: TwoDigitYear
| Text | Year | Notes                    |
| 24   | 2024 |                          |
| 05   | 2005 | The start of the history |
| 69   | 2069 | The top of the window    |
| 70   | 1970 | The bottom               |
| 99   | 1999 |                          |

Attributes TwoDigitYear
| Attribute | Type    | Default |
| Text      | String  |         |
| Year      | Integer | 2000    |
| Notes     | String  | none    |

Scenario A file whose dates could be read either way is reported before import
Description Every date in this file has a first part of twelve or less, so both
\ readings work and they disagree. The import says which it will use
\ and what the file would look like the other way, and waits.
Given the csv profile is : CsvProfile Vertical
| Name         | Ambiguous Bank |
| DateColumn   | Date           |
| DateOrder    | MDY            |
| PayeeColumn  | Description    |
| AmountStyle  | Signed         |
| AmountColumn | Amount         |
Given the file contains
"""
Date,Description,Amount
03/04/2024,SHOP A,-10.00
05/06/2024,SHOP B,-20.00
"""
Then date order is ambiguous : DateOrderWarning Vertical
| Ambiguous      | true       |
| UsingOrder     | MDY        |
| FirstDateAsMDY | 2024-03-04 |
| FirstDateAsDMY | 2024-04-03 |

Attributes DateOrderWarning
| Attribute      | Type      | Default    |
| Ambiguous      | Boolean   | false      |
| UsingOrder     | DateOrder | MDY        |
| FirstDateAsMDY | Date      | 2024-01-01 |
| FirstDateAsDMY | Date      | 2024-01-01 |

Scenario A file with an unambiguous date is not queried
Description One date with a first part above twelve settles the order for the
\ whole file, and it agrees with the profile, so nothing is asked.
Given the csv profile is : CsvProfile Vertical
