# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Duplicates, without an identifier to go on
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

DomainTerm Fingerprint : Text
Description The date and the amount of a row, with the payee compared as the
\ payee rule describes -- the rules first, then loosely. It is the name
\ for what two records of one transaction have in common when neither
\ carries an identifier.
Details The date and the amount are the key. The payee is not part of it,
\ because the first reconciliation against a history brought over from
\ Quicken would match nothing if it were: Harris Teeter against HARRIS
\ TEETER #231. What the payee decides is the note. See two payees are
\ compared after the rules and then loosely, in ImportOfx.

BusinessRule How a CSV row is tested against what is already there
Description The tests of ImportOfx, less the one about identifiers, which a CSV
\ file usually has nothing for. Where a file does carry a reference of
\ its own, test one applies to it and is scoped to that source.
Details There was a weaker rule here once, and it was weaker in both
\ directions. It offered a row that matched rather than settling it, so
\ re-reading a file asked about every row of it; and it reached five
\ days on an amount alone, which is a guess about whether somebody
\ shopped twice dressed up as a match. The rule now is the one rule,
\ stated once in ImportOfx and read here for a file without
\ identifiers.
Examples: CsvDispositionRule
| Test | Condition                                                             | Disposition |
| 1    | A transaction in this account carries this row's reference, from a Csv import | Duplicate |
| 2    | This is a checking account, and a transaction in it has the same check number, amount and payee, dated within the book's check-number window | Duplicate |
| 3    | The rows and the transactions in this account with this date and this amount are equal in number | Duplicate |
| 4    | There are transactions in this account with this date and this amount, and they are not equal in number to the rows | Possible |
| 5    | Nothing above applies                                                 | New         |

Attributes CsvDispositionRule
| Attribute   | Type              | Default |
| Test        | Integer           | 0       |
| Condition   | String            |         |
| Disposition | ImportDisposition | New     |

BusinessRule How many rows match how many transactions
Description The tests above ask whether something matching is there. That is not
\ enough, because it answers the same way however many there are. Two
\ rows in one file with the same date and amount are two transactions,
\ not one row read twice, so the file and the book are counted against
\ each other.
Details Where the counts agree, every one of them is a duplicate. Two rows
\ and two transactions is one file read twice; nothing is created and
\ both transactions are cleared. Where the counts do not agree, every
\ one of them is a question: nothing says which row goes with which
\ transaction, and there is no reason to prefer one pairing over
\ another.
\
\ Pairing them off one for one and calling the remainder new was the
\ earlier rule, and it decided the one thing nobody can decide from a
\ file. Two rows against one transaction is either a second visit to
\ the shop or a file that has been read before and a transaction that
\ was missed; the counts say which only when they agree. So the
\ disagreeing case asks, and asks about all of it, because the row that
\ would have been paired off is no better evidenced than the row that
\ would have been left over.
\
\ The test that asks only "is there one like this?" silently loses the
\ second coffee of the day, the second fuel stop, the second identical
\ transfer. Nothing reports it, the register is simply short, and the
\ account reconciles against nothing. Of the two ways to be wrong this
\ is the worse one: an extra row is seen and deleted, a missing row is
\ discovered a year later or never.
\
\ The counting is per account, as every other duplicate test here is,
\ and over what tests one and two have not already settled: a row whose
\ reference or check number has named its transaction is out of the
\ count, and so is that transaction.
\
\ The book is never changed to fit the file. Where the book holds more
\ than the file does -- the same amount twice over where the download
\ has it once -- the spare is left alone and nothing is reported about
\ it. The file is a statement of what the bank knows, not an
\ instruction to make the book match.
Examples: ClaimPairing
| InFile | AlreadyThere | Duplicate | Possible | New | Notes                                   |
| 1      | 0            | 0         | 0        | 1   | Nothing to match                        |
| 1      | 1            | 1         | 0        | 0   | One for one                             |
| 2      | 0            | 0         | 0        | 2   | Two genuine visits, neither seen before |
| 2      | 1            | 0         | 2        | 0   | The counts disagree, so both are asked  |
| 2      | 2            | 2         | 0        | 0   | Two for two: the file read twice        |
| 3      | 2            | 0         | 3        | 0   | The counts disagree                     |
| 1      | 2            | 0         | 1        | 0   | The counts disagree; the spare is still left alone |

Attributes ClaimPairing
| Attribute    | Type    | Default |
| InFile       | Integer | 0       |
| AlreadyThere | Integer | 0       |
| Duplicate    | Integer | 0       |
