Scenario Two identical rows against one transaction already there
Description The case the rule exists for. Two coffees at the same shop on the
\ same day for the same amount, and the book has one of them: either
\ the second was never entered, or this file has been read before and
\ one of the two was missed. Nothing in the file says which, so both
\ rows are asked about.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking        | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Uncategorized | Expense | false       |
Given transactions are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Payee      | Destination            | Amount |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2026-03-02 | Joe Coffee | Expenses:Uncategorized | -4.75  |
Given the csv profile is : CsvProfile Vertical
| Name           | Quicken Export |
| DateColumn     | Date           |
| PayeeColumn    | Payee          |
| AmountStyle    | Signed         |
| AmountColumn   | Amount         |
Given the file contains
"""
Date,Payee,Amount
03/02/2026,Joe Coffee,-4.75
03/02/2026,Joe Coffee,-4.75
"""
Given import target is : ImportTarget Vertical
| AccountPath | Assets:Checking |
When rows imported
Then import summary is : ImportSummary Vertical
| Possible | 2 |
| New      | 0 |
# Two rows against one transaction: the counts disagree, so both are offered
# and neither is decided. Pairing one of them off and calling the other new
# would be choosing between two readings of the same file on no evidence.

