Scenario Re-importing a file only half of which was accepted asks about the other half
Description The remaining case of reading a file twice. Three rows were
\ accepted and two were left unticked, so the book has three of the
\ five. The second read has to say Duplicate three times and New
\ twice, and it does, because the counting is per date and amount
\ rather than per file.
Details This is the shape the author's report was about -- the same file
\ read a second time -- and it is the one that cannot be got right by
\ remembering which files have been read. A file is not a unit of
\ anything after the review: what the book has is whatever was
\ ticked, and the second import has to work that out from the book.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking        | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Uncategorized | Expense | false       |
Given transactions are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Payee         | Destination            | Amount |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2024-01-15 | Harris Teeter | Expenses:Uncategorized | -45.00 |
| T2  | Assets:Checking | 2024-01-16 | Shell         | Expenses:Uncategorized | -52.00 |
| T3  | Assets:Checking | 2024-01-17 | Joe Coffee    | Expenses:Uncategorized | -4.75  |
Given the csv profile is : CsvProfile Vertical
| Name         | Quicken Export |
| DateColumn   | Date           |
| PayeeColumn  | Payee          |
| AmountStyle  | Signed         |
| AmountColumn | Amount         |
Given the file contains
"""
Date,Payee,Amount
01/15/2024,Harris Teeter,-45.00
01/16/2024,Shell,-52.00
01/17/2024,Joe Coffee,-4.75
01/18/2024,Harris Teeter,-31.20
01/19/2024,Shell,-48.00
"""
Given import target is : ImportTarget Vertical
| AccountPath | Assets:Checking |
When rows imported
Then import summary is : ImportSummary Vertical
| New       | 2 |
| Duplicate | 3 |
| Possible  | 0 |
# Nothing is Possible: each of the three has one row and one transaction, so
# the counts agree date by date and amount by amount.

BusinessRule What a fingerprint ignores
Description Two rows that differ only in how the payee is spaced, cased or
\ numbered are the same row. Two that differ in amount or date are not.
Details The last row is the one that moved. A trailing number on a payee is a
\ store number or a terminal number, not a different shop, and treating
\ it as a difference put a payee note on every row of a reconciliation
\ against a Quicken history. See two payees are compared after the
\ rules and then loosely, in ImportOfx: this is that comparison, named
\ from the CSV side.
Examples: FingerprintMatch
| DateA      | PayeeA          | AmountA | DateB      | PayeeB          | AmountB | Same  |
| 2024-01-15 | Harris Teeter   | -45.00  | 2024-01-15 | HARRIS TEETER   | -45.00  | true  |
| 2024-01-15 | Harris  Teeter  | -45.00  | 2024-01-15 | Harris Teeter   | -45.00  | true  |
| 2024-01-15 | Harris Teeter   | -45.00  | 2024-01-15 | Harris Teeter   | -45.01  | false |
| 2024-01-15 | Harris Teeter   | -45.00  | 2024-01-16 | Harris Teeter   | -45.00  | false |
| 2024-01-15 | Harris Teeter   | -45.00  | 2024-01-15 | Harris Teeter 2 | -45.00  | true  |

Attributes FingerprintMatch
| Attribute | Type      | Default    |
| DateA     | Date      | 2024-01-01 |
| PayeeA    | PayeeName | none       |
| AmountA   | Money     | 0.00       |
| DateB     | Date      | 2024-01-01 |
| PayeeB    | PayeeName | none       |
| AmountB   | Money     | 0.00       |
| Same      | Boolean   | false      |
